<h1>STAR Interviewer Guide</h1>
<p>A concise reference for hiring managers and interviewers using the STAR behavioral method.</p>
<hr/>
<h2>Before the Interview</h2>
<ul><li>[ ] Review the role's key competencies and their behavioral anchors.</li><li>[ ] Prepare 4-6 STAR behavioral questions mapped to the competencies you are assessing.</li><li>[ ] Review the candidate's resume — note specific experiences to probe.</li><li>[ ] Block your calendar; no interruptions during the interview.</li><li>[ ] Open a blank scorecard to take notes in real time.</li></ul>
<hr/>
<h2>Interview Structure</h2>
<h3>Opening (5 min)</h3>
<ul><li>Introduce yourself and your role.</li><li>Explain the format: &quot;I'll be asking behavioral questions — I'm interested in specific situations from your past experience.&quot;</li><li>Set expectations: &quot;I may ask follow-up questions to get more detail. That's normal, not a red flag.&quot;</li></ul>
<h3>STAR Questions (40 min)</h3>
<p>For each question:</p>
<ol><li><strong>Ask</strong> the behavioral question. Wait for the full answer before probing.</li><li><strong>Listen</strong> for all four STAR components as the candidate speaks.</li><li><strong>Probe</strong> for missing components:<ul><li>Missing Situation: &quot;Can you give me more context about when this happened?&quot;</li><li>Missing Task: &quot;What was your specific responsibility in that situation?&quot;</li><li>Missing Action: &quot;Walk me through exactly what YOU did — what steps did you take?&quot;</li><li>Missing Result: &quot;What was the outcome? Do you have any numbers to share?&quot;</li></ul></li><li><strong>Note</strong> specific actions and results, not impressions or adjectives.</li></ol>
<h3>Candidate Questions (10 min)</h3>
<ul><li>Allow the candidate to ask questions.</li><li>Answer honestly; do not oversell.</li></ul>
<h3>Closing (5 min)</h3>
<ul><li>Explain next steps and timeline.</li><li>Thank the candidate.</li></ul>
<hr/>
<h2>After the Interview</h2>
<ul><li>[ ] Complete the scorecard immediately after the interview — not hours later.</li><li>[ ] Submit the scorecard before the debrief meeting.</li><li>[ ] Record specific examples and quotes, not general impressions.</li><li>[ ] Do not discuss your assessment with other interviewers until the debrief.</li></ul>
<hr/>
<h2>Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>Why It Fails</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Accepting &quot;we&quot; answers without probing</td><td>Cannot score personal contribution</td></tr><tr><td>Letting a compelling story skip the Result probe</td><td>Outcome is the strongest scoring signal</td></tr><tr><td>Sharing your score before debrief</td><td>Anchors other interviewers to your view</td></tr><tr><td>Taking notes on impressions (&quot;seemed nervous&quot;)</td><td>Not scorable evidence</td></tr><tr><td>Asking hypothetical instead of behavioral</td><td>&quot;What would you do&quot; predicts less than &quot;what did you do&quot;</td></tr></tbody></table>
